<Base title="Privacy policy" description="fibub never uploads, stores or sees your files. We keep only anonymous daily totals of tool use, with no IP addresses or visitor IDs.">
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>Privacy policy</h1>
    <p class="lead">Your files never leave your device, and we store nothing about you. We only count how often the tools are used, in anonymous daily totals that anyone can see on the <a href="/stats/">usage stats page</a>.</p>

    <h2>Your files and what you type</h2>
    <p>Every tool runs inside your web browser, on your own device. Files you choose are read by your browser and are never uploaded to fibub or anyone else. The smaller file is made on your device and saved straight from it. We never see, store, share or use your files, their names or their contents. You can check this yourself: open a tool, switch off your internet, and it keeps working.</p>

    <h2>What we count</h2>
    <p>When a tool finishes, your browser sends one anonymous note so we can see which tools are useful and fix what fails. It contains only:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>which tool was used, the kind of file (for example "PDF") and a size range (for example "1 to 5 MB")</li>
      <li>whether it worked, and how much smaller the file became</li>
      <li>the type of device (phone, tablet or computer), operating system and browser name</li>
      <li>the country, worked out by our host from the connection; the IP address itself is never stored</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Each note is added straight into a daily total and then discarded, so no single visit can be picked out later. We do not record the time of day, and we do not use cookies, visitor IDs, fingerprinting or any way to recognise you when you come back. The daily totals are published openly on the <a href="/stats/">usage stats page</a> and in the site's public code repository.</p>

    <h2>Page views and tool uses</h2>
    <p>The home page shows how many pages have been opened on fibub, and each page shows how many times it has been opened, under its title. To keep these counts, your browser sends one tiny note when a page opens, saying only which page it is, and one more the first time you use a tool on that page. Each note just adds one to that day's total and to the running total. Nothing else is sent or kept: no IP address, no time of day, no device or browser details, no cookie and no ID, so the totals cannot be traced to anyone. Reloading a page counts again, because we keep no way to tell whether you have been here before. The daily totals for every page are published openly in the <a href="https://github.com/nihalunfc/fibub/tree/main/data/views">site's public code repository</a>.</p>

    <h2>Cookies and storage</h2>
    <p>fibub sets no cookies. A few choices are kept in your browser's own storage on your device and never sent to us: light or dark mode (only if you pick dark), the tools you star as My tools, and settings or work in tools that say so, such as "Remember my calendar". Red light lasts only while the tab is open. To let pages work offline, and to let you <a href="/app/">install fibub as an app</a>, your browser also keeps copies of the fibub pages you open and the files they need; these copies hold only fibub's own pages, never your files or what you type, and are never sent anywhere. Clearing your browser's site data removes all of it.</p>

    <h2>Our host</h2>
    <p>The site is served by Cloudflare, which protects it from attacks. Like any web host, Cloudflare processes your IP address to deliver pages and may keep short-lived security logs under <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">its own privacy policy</a>. We do not use those logs to track anyone.</p>

    <h2>Advertising</h2>
    <p>fibub shows no ads today. If that changes, this page will be updated first, and pages that handle your files will stay free of ad code.</p>

    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>fibub collects no personal information from anyone, including children.</p>

    <h2>Your rights</h2>
    <p>Because we hold no personal information about you, there is nothing for us to give back, correct or delete. If you have a question about privacy, including under Canada's PIPEDA, Quebec's Law 25, the UK GDPR, the EU GDPR, India's DPDP Act or the privacy laws of the Gulf states, <a href="/contact/">contact us</a>.</p>

    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <p>If this policy changes, the new version will be posted here with a new date.</p>
    <p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:14px">Last updated 2026-11-13.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
